1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,760
On Venus, a single day lasts
longer than a whole year.

2
00:00:04,125 --> 00:00:07,615
Venus circles the Sun once every
two hundred and twenty-five

3
00:00:07,625 --> 00:00:08,605
Earth days.

4
00:00:09,083 --> 00:00:12,498
But turning once on its axis
takes two hundred and

5
00:00:12,508 --> 00:00:13,073
forty-three.

6
00:00:13,083 --> 00:00:16,393
So it finishes a lap of the Sun
before it has spun around

7
00:00:16,403 --> 00:00:17,203
even once.

8
00:00:17,625 --> 00:00:20,835
And it spins backwards, the
opposite way to almost

9
00:00:20,845 --> 00:00:21,845
every other planet.

10
00:00:22,250 --> 00:00:25,540
Stand on Venus, and the Sun
would rise in the west and

11
00:00:25,550 --> 00:00:26,350
set in the east.

12
00:00:26,792 --> 00:00:28,212
Nobody is sure why.

13
00:00:28,832 --> 00:00:32,222
A giant impact may have flipped
it, or the Sun's pull on

14
00:00:32,232 --> 00:00:34,092
its thick atmosphere may have
slowed it.

15
00:00:34,375 --> 00:00:37,345
Spinning backwards does shorten
sunrise to sunrise,

16
00:00:37,355 --> 00:00:39,835
to about a hundred and seventeen
days.

17
00:00:40,333 --> 00:00:43,293
Still, one spin that takes
longer than a whole orbit.

18
00:00:43,913 --> 00:00:45,953
Venus really is the odd one out.
